WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.019
"What Are the Best Foods?"

00:00:11.019 --> 00:00:14.960
Instead of just looking at individual
studies, or individual reviews

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:19.789
of studies, what if you looked
at a review of reviews?

00:00:19.789 --> 00:00:22.750
In my last video
I covered the beverages,

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:25.860
with the majority of reviews that
found some effects either way,

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:29.309
finding at least some benefits
to tea, coffee, wine, and milk

00:00:29.309 --> 00:00:32.770
but not to sweetened
beverages such as soda.

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:35.710
As I explored in depth,
this approach isn't perfect.

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:38.930
It doesn't take into account
things like conflicts of interest

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:42.750
such as industry funding of studies
but can offer an interesting

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:47.350
bird's eye view on what's out
there in the medical literature.

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:50.540
What did the data show for food groups?

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:54.290
You'll note the first thing
the authors did was split it up

00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:57.740
into plant-based foods
and animal foods.

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:01.850
For the broadest takeaway
we can look at the totals.

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:05.170
The vast majority of studies on whole
plant foods show either protective

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:09.549
or, at the very least, neutral effects,
whereas most reviews of animal-based

00:01:09.549 --> 00:01:15.380
foods identified deleterious health
effects or, at best, neutral effects.

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:20.100
Let's break these down, though.
The plant-foods consistently rate

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:26.009
uniformly well, reflecting the total, but
the animal foods vary considerably.

00:01:26.009 --> 00:01:28.260
As you can see, if it wasn't
for dairy and fish,

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:32.369
the animal foods total would swing
almost entirely neutral or negative.

00:01:32.369 --> 00:01:36.229
I talked about the effects of dairy
industry funding in my last video,

00:01:36.229 --> 00:01:39.049
as well as substitution effects.

00:01:39.049 --> 00:01:42.759
Those who drink milk may be
less likely to drink soda,

00:01:42.759 --> 00:01:46.249
a beverage even more
universally condemned,

00:01:46.249 --> 00:01:49.020
so the protective effects
may be relative, arising

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:50.479
not necessarily from
what they're consuming

00:01:50.479 --> 00:01:56.700
but rather from what they're avoiding.
This may best explain the fish findings.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:58.380
After all, the prototypical choice

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:02.630
is between chicken and fish,
not chicken and chickpeas.

00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:09.090
And not a single review found a single
protective effect of poultry consumption.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:13.790
Even the soda industry could come
up with 14% protective effects,

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:16.959
but despite all the funding from
the National Chicken Council

00:02:16.959 --> 00:02:23.629
and the American Egg Board, chicken
and eggs got big fat goose eggs.

00:02:23.629 --> 00:02:26.230
Also, like the calcium in dairy,

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:29.909
there are healthful components of fish:
the long-chain omega-3 fatty acids.

00:02:29.909 --> 00:02:34.000
Not for heart health. In the most
extensive systematic assessment

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:38.159
of effects of omega-3 fats on
cardiovascular health to date,

00:02:38.159 --> 00:02:42.460
increasing the fish oil fats had little
or no effect on cardiovascular health.

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:45.500
In fact, if anything, it was
the plant-based omega-3s

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:48.760
found in flaxseeds and
walnuts that was protective.

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:52.170
But the long-chain omega-3s
are important for brain health.

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:55.760
Thankfully, just like there are
best-of-both-worlds non-dairy sources

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:00.530
of calcium, there are pollutant-free
sources of the long chain omega-3s,

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:02.460
EPA and DHA, as well.

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:07.610
The bottom line is that when it comes to
diet-related diseases such as obesity,

00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:11.819
type 2 diabetes, mental health,
bone health, cardiovascular disease,

00:03:11.819 --> 00:03:15.390
and cancers, even if you lump
all the animal foods together

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:21.780
and ignore any industry funding effects,
and just take the existing body of

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:25.920
evidence at face value, nine out
of ten study compilations show that

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:30.110
whole plant foods are, in the very least,
not bad, whereas about eight out of ten

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:34.600
of the reviews on animal products
show them to be not good.

